WEBVTT

00:00:01.523 --> 00:00:05.128
It seems today
 that all you see

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.131
Is violence in movies
 and sex on TV

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:11.893
But where are those
 good old-fashioned values

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.821
On which we used to rely?

00:00:14.898 --> 00:00:18.083
Lucky there's a family guy

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.811
Lucky there's a man who
 positively can do

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:23.313
All the things that make us

00:00:23.315 --> 00:00:24.906
Laugh and cry

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:30.387
He's... a...
 Fam... ily... Guy!

00:00:34.051 --> 00:00:37.201
Let's see, Hemingway,
Tolstoy, Dickens.

00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:38.795
Oh, there's my iPad.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.205
Thank God. Some nerd
must've been using it.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:42.217
Hi, everybody.

00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:44.951
You know how when you go on Hulu
to watch Family Guy,

00:00:44.953 --> 00:00:47.120
you got to scroll past
a bunch of little boxes?

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.306
Well, it turns out,
those are other TV shows.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:51.935
TV shows that I'd never heard of

00:00:52.138 --> 00:00:54.477
until they step up onstage
to accept their Emmy,

00:00:54.604 --> 00:00:56.029
for which we were not nominated.

00:00:56.105 --> 00:00:58.031
Well, tonight, we're gonna
click on three of them.

00:00:58.107 --> 00:01:02.073
This first one has Steve Martin,
Martin Short and Selena Gomez.

00:01:02.132 --> 00:01:05.355
So, if you were born
in 1950 or 2005,

00:01:05.357 --> 00:01:06.977
you're gonna see
your favorite actor.

00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:09.736
Now, please enjoy
 Only Murders in the Building.

00:01:18.962 --> 00:01:22.147
Drink it. It's just pop.

00:01:22.419 --> 00:01:23.817
There's a pill on top.

00:01:25.635 --> 00:01:27.569
Oh, wait, it's gone now.

00:01:32.976 --> 00:01:35.712
No, no, no, no. This isn't
a dark Netflix show, all right?

00:01:35.737 --> 00:01:38.163
Our murders are solved
by the Father of the Bride.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.406
Well, what am I supposed to do?

00:01:40.431 --> 00:01:42.186
I don't know, maybe learn
to be a better host.

00:01:42.211 --> 00:01:44.199
All four of your guests
are sound asleep.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.256
-Hello, Charles.
-Hello, Oliver.

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:51.759
This is the type of banter
the show's known for.

00:01:53.521 --> 00:01:55.004
-Who's that?
-I don't know, but she's got

00:01:55.006 --> 00:01:58.116
430 million followers
on Instagram.

00:01:58.141 --> 00:02:00.124
Ah. What's Instagram?

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:02.670
What's Insta-- How on earth
are we on a streamer?

00:02:04.292 --> 00:02:06.199
Brr. Ugh, let me just shake off

00:02:06.201 --> 00:02:07.236
this obviously fake,

00:02:07.261 --> 00:02:09.352
"only on the shoulders"
New York snow.

00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:11.915
You guys all coming from
the Statue of Liberty, too?

00:02:14.664 --> 00:02:17.815
Man, what a great day
to be and stay alive.

00:02:18.029 --> 00:02:20.388
Wow, really?
Back-to-back elevator scenes?

00:02:20.413 --> 00:02:23.132
Yeah, this show is,
like, 40% elevator.

00:02:23.732 --> 00:02:24.777
Well, good night.

00:02:27.732 --> 00:02:29.630
My goodness, what happened here?

00:02:29.708 --> 00:02:32.709
Sorry, folks, there was
an only murder in this building.

00:02:32.711 --> 00:02:35.469
Ooh, a murder.
I've got goosebumps.

00:02:35.547 --> 00:02:37.397
-Me, too.
-Yeah, those are shingles.

00:02:37.473 --> 00:02:40.066
You're 70. I told you
to get the shot, you dope.

00:02:40.939 --> 00:02:43.069
Well, looks like they don't want
people meddling in there.

00:02:43.244 --> 00:02:45.555
What do you think,
should we banjo our way in?

00:02:45.557 --> 00:02:48.500
No, no, no, no, that's
for your time, not our time.

00:02:50.729 --> 00:02:52.078
He had one inside.

00:02:52.155 --> 00:02:54.798
It looks like someone
tried to eat him.

00:02:56.318 --> 00:02:57.917
Do you think it was someone
in the building?

00:02:57.994 --> 00:02:59.402
I think I know who did it.

00:02:59.404 --> 00:03:02.238
It must've been my creepy
next-door neighbor, Jeff.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.499
Dahmer? Jeffy D.? Really?
But he's so cool.

00:03:05.524 --> 00:03:06.632
You don't understand,

00:03:06.702 --> 00:03:08.961
I can hear screams
coming from his apartment.

00:03:09.097 --> 00:03:11.338
Look, Glenda,
we appreciate your concern,

00:03:11.416 --> 00:03:13.007
but Jeff is just
a harmless weirdo.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.859
So what if he watches
a lot of horror movies

00:03:14.936 --> 00:03:16.586
and cooks a lot of pork chops?

00:03:16.588 --> 00:03:18.846
We all picked up weird hobbies
over the pandemic.

00:03:18.924 --> 00:03:21.591
I'm telling you,
there's something bad

00:03:21.593 --> 00:03:23.426
going on in apartment 2B.

00:03:23.428 --> 00:03:24.944
Uh-huh.
Look, thanks for stopping by,

00:03:25.021 --> 00:03:26.520
but why don't you
leave this investigation

00:03:26.598 --> 00:03:27.947
to the non-professionals?

00:03:28.024 --> 00:03:30.709
Hey, didn't you get reprimanded
by the co-op board

00:03:30.785 --> 00:03:33.044
for being inappropriate
with the doorman?

00:03:33.121 --> 00:03:35.288
The third-party arbitrator
said it's not illegal

00:03:35.364 --> 00:03:38.274
to tell someone when you're
going to the gym. Good day.

00:03:38.276 --> 00:03:41.368
Question: Should we exploit
this tragedy with a podcast?

00:03:41.446 --> 00:03:42.515
Somebody say "podcast"?

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.630
If you guys are doing
a murder pod, I want in.

00:03:44.632 --> 00:03:45.873
Whoa, whoa, whoa,
what makes you think

00:03:45.951 --> 00:03:48.134
you're qualified
to make a murder podcast?

00:03:48.322 --> 00:03:51.137
I'm a millennial woman
with nothing else going on.

00:03:51.214 --> 00:03:52.472
Uh, crap, she's got us there.

00:03:52.548 --> 00:03:53.957
Bland, 30-year-old women

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:56.550
are the Navy SEALs
of murder podcasting.

00:03:56.954 --> 00:03:58.461
What do you think?
Do we include her?

00:03:58.669 --> 00:03:59.796
Well, we do need someone

00:03:59.798 --> 00:04:01.973
- who can turn the computer on.
- You're in.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.304
All right, our neighbor
was murdered in cold blood,

00:04:08.306 --> 00:04:09.489
and he deserves justice.

00:04:09.542 --> 00:04:10.546
There's no time to waste.

00:04:10.644 --> 00:04:13.636
We must decide what our
podcast voices are going to be.

00:04:13.817 --> 00:04:14.882
I was thinking of...

00:04:15.278 --> 00:04:16.015
taking...

00:04:16.664 --> 00:04:18.457
unnatural pauses where...

00:04:19.249 --> 00:04:21.311
normal people don't...
take them.

00:04:21.571 --> 00:04:24.578
Yeah, I thought I could speak
way too close to the microphone

00:04:24.656 --> 00:04:26.598
with a very wet mouth.

00:04:27.937 --> 00:04:28.658
Mm.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.084
I can be the female
on the podcast

00:04:30.161 --> 00:04:32.328
who laughs at everything
the guys say.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:33.663
Do they always
have one of those?

00:04:35.286 --> 00:04:36.833
That's hilarious.

00:04:36.851 --> 00:04:39.194
We're good to go.

00:04:42.023 --> 00:04:44.115
-Where do we start?
-Well, wasn't Glenda

00:04:44.192 --> 00:04:45.450
trying to tell us
something about Jeff?

00:04:45.627 --> 00:04:47.051
Maybe we should
follow up on that.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:50.054
Jeffrey Dahmer? Of course.
He always has great ideas.

00:04:50.131 --> 00:04:51.872
That's right.
He sent that great email

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.874
about how to get blood stains
out of your power tools.

00:04:53.952 --> 00:04:55.226
Let's see what he thinks.

00:04:57.305 --> 00:04:59.472
Hi. Oh, uh, sorry.

00:05:00.076 --> 00:05:01.566
I was... making pork chops.

00:05:01.643 --> 00:05:03.142
Hey, Jeff. So sorry to bug you,

00:05:03.196 --> 00:05:06.129
but you haven't heard anything
about Tim's murder, have you?

00:05:06.131 --> 00:05:08.981
What...? Tim's dead?

00:05:09.134 --> 00:05:11.576
But he was so delicious.
I mean, young.

00:05:11.578 --> 00:05:14.228
I mean, I-I haven't
heard anything.

00:05:14.441 --> 00:05:17.415
Aw, nuts. Well, we're worried
the killer might strike again,

00:05:17.492 --> 00:05:19.417
so here's a list
of vulnerable people

00:05:19.442 --> 00:05:20.593
in the building who live alone.

00:05:20.618 --> 00:05:21.804
Would you mind
keeping an eye on them?

00:05:21.961 --> 00:05:23.162
A lot of them
don't have families.

00:05:23.314 --> 00:05:25.656
If something happened to them,
they would not be missed.

00:05:25.851 --> 00:05:27.455
Uh, yeah, sure.

00:05:27.532 --> 00:05:31.055
I will definitely do
something like that.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.991
Say, anybody have a spare
bone saw charger I could borrow?

00:05:34.993 --> 00:05:37.584
Ugh, man, those things
are so easy to lose.

00:05:37.662 --> 00:05:39.095
If I had a nickel, you know?

00:05:39.588 --> 00:05:42.025
I do. I know about nickels.

00:05:42.433 --> 00:05:43.708
Uh, I see your naked roommate

00:05:43.785 --> 00:05:45.025
is trying to climb out
the window.

00:05:45.103 --> 00:05:46.377
Looks like you've got
your hands full.

00:05:46.454 --> 00:05:48.862
If you hear anything,
give us a ring-a-ding-ding, eh?

00:05:50.275 --> 00:05:53.885
Are you out of your minds?
Jeffrey is obviously the killer.

00:05:53.961 --> 00:05:55.533
Didn't you see
the blood on his shirt?

00:05:55.558 --> 00:05:57.889
And look at the garbage
he left in the hallway.

00:05:59.617 --> 00:06:02.618
So he's a little messy. That's
no reason to point fingers.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.136
I don't believe this.

00:06:04.213 --> 00:06:07.214
All the clues are right
in front of you. Can't you see?

00:06:07.441 --> 00:06:10.068
You know what I see?
I see a hostile neighbor

00:06:10.144 --> 00:06:12.904
who's emotionally unstable
and prone to outbursts.

00:06:13.244 --> 00:06:16.557
Why, that fits the profile
of a murderer.

00:06:21.545 --> 00:06:23.874
Look how many people
are out here in bathrobes.

00:06:24.049 --> 00:06:25.291
I know it's the middle
of the night,

00:06:25.293 --> 00:06:26.976
but, come on, make an effort.

00:06:26.978 --> 00:06:28.402
You're making a mistake.

00:06:28.479 --> 00:06:30.905
The murderer is obviously
Jeffrey Dahmer,

00:06:31.079 --> 00:06:32.505
and he'll kill again.

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:38.579
No, no, no, no.
Not Netflix. Hulu ending.

00:06:41.659 --> 00:06:43.584
Statue of Liberty, my good man.

00:06:44.676 --> 00:06:46.845
So, Jeff,
is there a Mrs. Dahmer?

00:06:46.972 --> 00:06:48.232
Ew. No.

00:06:48.593 --> 00:06:51.686
Oh, Jeff brought
suspiciously fizzy roadies.

00:06:51.803 --> 00:06:53.978
Here's to a murder well solved.

00:06:59.885 --> 00:07:02.828
New York, New York,
 it's a wonderful town

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:04.721
The Bronx is up,
 and the Battery's down

00:07:04.799 --> 00:07:06.157
The people ride
 in a hole in the ground.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:15.658
Hi, there. Our next show
answers the question:

00:07:15.734 --> 00:07:17.568
What if you could
walk into a drugstore

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:19.145
and test yourself for AIDS

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:22.315
right next to a shelf full
of leftover Valentine's candy?

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:25.344
Ladies and gentlemen,
please enjoy The Dropout.

00:07:28.725 --> 00:07:31.284
So, you can probably tell
from my Razor scooter

00:07:31.326 --> 00:07:32.374
that I'm in tech.

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.102
I even got a scooter
for the building.

00:07:36.328 --> 00:07:40.090
Yes, of course, and I love
your hip, modern offices.

00:07:40.368 --> 00:07:42.718
Thanks. I didn't want
my employees' ideas

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:44.478
constrained by old paradigms

00:07:44.555 --> 00:07:47.148
like "walls" and "desks."

00:07:49.043 --> 00:07:50.634
So, what are your
qualifications?

00:07:50.711 --> 00:07:53.765
Well, I dropped out
of Stanford after six days.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:54.973
All I heard was Stanford.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.733
Now, this idea of yours,
does it "rupt"?

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.885
No. It disrupts.

00:07:59.887 --> 00:08:01.053
Tell me everything.

00:08:01.072 --> 00:08:04.073
Well, I believe every person
deserves to know

00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:06.392
which Sex and the City
character they are.

00:08:06.394 --> 00:08:08.894
And until now,
the only way to find that out

00:08:08.896 --> 00:08:11.246
was to either draw
a full gallon of blood

00:08:11.248 --> 00:08:13.248
or take a lengthy BuzzFeed quiz.

00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:14.842
You mean, there's a better way?

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:18.253
With just one drop of blood,
my device can tell you

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.163
if you're a Samantha, a Miranda,

00:08:20.241 --> 00:08:22.741
or even Magda,
the uptight housekeeper.

00:08:22.743 --> 00:08:24.167
Ms. Holmes, I can easily say

00:08:24.245 --> 00:08:26.187
that's the worst idea
I've ever heard.

00:08:26.263 --> 00:08:28.004
Oh, really? Well, what if I

00:08:28.082 --> 00:08:29.431
pitch it like this?

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:33.009
Oh, my God. This is a complete
game "stay the samer."

00:08:33.034 --> 00:08:34.976
I mean, game changer.
That-That's the good one.

00:08:35.092 --> 00:08:37.092
Tell you what,
if you pitch it one more time

00:08:37.258 --> 00:08:40.910
with your eyes really wide
like a lunatic owl, I'm in.

00:08:42.705 --> 00:08:44.372
Science.

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:50.701
I'm thinking maybe one wall.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.459
Thank you all for joining me
on this exciting journey.

00:08:57.767 --> 00:09:00.786
And you're all here
on the ground floor.

00:09:00.965 --> 00:09:03.057
I decided to name the company

00:09:03.059 --> 00:09:05.559
after our two
founding principles:

00:09:05.629 --> 00:09:08.155
quizzes and diagnosis.

00:09:10.141 --> 00:09:12.233
-I'm so excited!
-This is going great.

00:09:12.309 --> 00:09:13.809
I helped set up the projector.

00:09:13.811 --> 00:09:16.219
Our mission
is to develop a machine

00:09:16.297 --> 00:09:18.722
that can run diagnostic tests

00:09:18.799 --> 00:09:20.999
with just one drop of blood.

00:09:21.001 --> 00:09:22.818
I got it to work with two drops.

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:24.653
Two drops is garbage.
You're fired.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:25.905
Get out of my office.

00:09:27.642 --> 00:09:29.083
Now, I don't have to tell you

00:09:29.198 --> 00:09:32.174
just how groundbreaking it is
for a woman

00:09:32.247 --> 00:09:36.008
to be running a company
like this in Silicon Valley.

00:09:36.121 --> 00:09:38.000
Sorry I'm late,
I was disrupting the toilet.

00:09:38.076 --> 00:09:39.427
Don't mind me,
I don't even work here,

00:09:39.462 --> 00:09:40.836
but I am now your king.

00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:42.465
Everyone, this is Sunny.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:46.267
He's a middle-aged man I met
in India when I was a teenager.

00:09:46.505 --> 00:09:50.438
He is my father figure,
business partner, mentor, lover,

00:09:50.514 --> 00:09:53.515
-and we share a Hulu password.
-Uhp, uhp, uhp, uhp, uhp, uhp.

00:09:53.592 --> 00:09:56.835
Like I was saying, Elizabeth
is definitely in charge.

00:09:56.837 --> 00:09:59.004
Also, we're pivoting away
from Sex and the City

00:09:59.058 --> 00:10:00.741
and doing Sopranos
characters instead.

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:01.782
What? No, n--

00:10:01.859 --> 00:10:04.434
No. No. No. That's not--

00:10:04.512 --> 00:10:06.436
All right, back to work.
Now, if you'll excuse me,

00:10:06.514 --> 00:10:08.697
I'm gonna google
where to put your outside leg

00:10:08.722 --> 00:10:10.297
during office couch sex.

00:10:10.617 --> 00:10:13.018
Oh, of course.
Never would've thought of that.

00:10:13.121 --> 00:10:15.736
Elizabeth, can I see you on
the office couch for a moment?

00:10:22.499 --> 00:10:25.433
How's the Sopranos
blood profiler coming along?

00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:26.699
Still not working.

00:10:26.845 --> 00:10:29.870
It says I'm the horny priest
who has lunch with Carmela,

00:10:29.895 --> 00:10:32.638
and... that's just...
th-there's no way.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.565
But we got the big presentation
for Walgreens tomorrow.

00:10:35.643 --> 00:10:37.567
This isn't CVS,
where they throw you out

00:10:37.645 --> 00:10:39.736
for staring at the
Maybelline posters too long.

00:10:39.814 --> 00:10:42.072
Can you get it working
by tomorrow?

00:10:42.149 --> 00:10:44.091
Honestly, I'm a little
over my head here.

00:10:44.168 --> 00:10:45.926
I applied
for a receptionist job,

00:10:45.951 --> 00:10:47.784
and you guys put me back here.

00:10:47.988 --> 00:10:49.930
-What are we gonna do?
-We just need to get

00:10:50.007 --> 00:10:51.506
to the next round of funding.

00:10:51.732 --> 00:10:54.159
The machine
doesn't have to really work,

00:10:54.161 --> 00:10:56.252
they just have to think
it works.

00:10:56.421 --> 00:10:58.088
Are you saying we do fraud?

00:10:58.165 --> 00:10:59.848
No, no, no, it's not fraud

00:10:59.902 --> 00:11:02.698
because the machine works
in my head.

00:11:03.053 --> 00:11:05.237
If I think it works,
then it works.

00:11:05.313 --> 00:11:07.389
That's the Silicon Valley motto.

00:11:07.391 --> 00:11:10.058
Just let me and Elizabeth
worry about the presentation.

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:11.226
Now, if you'll excuse us,

00:11:11.245 --> 00:11:12.911
we're gonna go get in
our self-driving car

00:11:12.988 --> 00:11:15.321
and mow down dogs
all the way home.

00:11:19.236 --> 00:11:21.570
Well, thanks
for having us, Ms. Holmes.

00:11:21.572 --> 00:11:23.755
We think your product
might be a great addition

00:11:23.757 --> 00:11:25.073
to our chain of drugstores.

00:11:25.075 --> 00:11:26.758
Just keep in mind,
I may have to duck out

00:11:26.835 --> 00:11:29.186
if somebody needs to
get into the eyedrops case.

00:11:29.263 --> 00:11:31.337
Well, we won't take up
too much of your time.

00:11:31.415 --> 00:11:34.007
I'm sure you are going to find
our machine

00:11:34.084 --> 00:11:36.176
is a perfect fit
for your stores.

00:11:36.253 --> 00:11:37.586
Eh, I don't know about that.

00:11:37.588 --> 00:11:39.679
We already have a machine
from Dr. Scholl's

00:11:39.757 --> 00:11:42.090
that tells you whether or not
you need Dr. Scholl's.

00:11:42.092 --> 00:11:43.592
I don't see medical science

00:11:43.646 --> 00:11:45.421
getting much more sophisticated
than that.

00:11:45.836 --> 00:11:47.873
Gentlemen, people used to say

00:11:47.932 --> 00:11:49.781
it was impossible to run tests

00:11:49.806 --> 00:11:51.426
on a single drop of blood.

00:11:51.769 --> 00:11:54.102
Well, I fired those people
and hired new ones.

00:11:54.104 --> 00:11:56.130
And now, behold.

00:11:58.501 --> 00:12:00.551
Ooh! Science noise.

00:12:00.727 --> 00:12:02.653
With just one drop of blood,

00:12:02.729 --> 00:12:06.490
our device can tell you exactly
which Sopranos character

00:12:06.567 --> 00:12:09.493
you are without having
to take a dumb quiz

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:11.679
written by
a social media intern.

00:12:11.704 --> 00:12:12.795
Why Sopranos?

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.406
Because my company

00:12:14.482 --> 00:12:16.741
-is like a criminal enterprise.
-All right.

00:12:16.818 --> 00:12:18.393
And for no particular reason,

00:12:18.395 --> 00:12:21.897
I'm gonna be behind this curtain
for the entire demonstration.

00:12:21.915 --> 00:12:24.842
All we need
is a tiny blood sample.

00:12:27.237 --> 00:12:29.179
Ooh, it's doing stuff.

00:12:33.293 --> 00:12:35.744
This says, "Artie Bucco."
Who's that?

00:12:35.746 --> 00:12:37.521
- Bald guy at the restaurant.
- Oh, yeah.

00:12:37.598 --> 00:12:39.247
He and Tony
were friends growing up.

00:12:39.249 --> 00:12:40.840
Ah, right, right, right.

00:12:40.865 --> 00:12:43.032
I'm not sure
this machine is for us.

00:12:43.087 --> 00:12:45.845
Just a sec, let me, uh,
recalibrate something.

00:12:48.592 --> 00:12:52.685
"Tony." All right.
Now this is Walgreens.

00:12:55.599 --> 00:12:58.116
Members of the Quizznos family,

00:12:58.193 --> 00:13:01.879
thanks to you,
the device is a huge success.

00:13:01.955 --> 00:13:04.364
All the Walgreens
executives love it.

00:13:04.441 --> 00:13:06.274
In fact, the company ordered

00:13:06.276 --> 00:13:09.331
an additional 80,000 machines.

00:13:09.356 --> 00:13:12.372
I told them we'd have them ready
by 2:30 today.

00:13:12.449 --> 00:13:15.411
It's 2:05, so... chop-chop.

00:13:15.723 --> 00:13:18.562
Wait. I know why people
love the machine so much.

00:13:18.639 --> 00:13:21.214
It's giving out
too many false positives.

00:13:21.291 --> 00:13:22.457
Just look at the data.

00:13:22.459 --> 00:13:24.884
It's telling everyone
they're a Tony.

00:13:24.962 --> 00:13:25.865
Look, wh...

00:13:26.313 --> 00:13:28.221
when you're blazing new trails,

00:13:28.298 --> 00:13:29.889
you are bound to hit

00:13:29.967 --> 00:13:31.800
a few speed bumps along the way.

00:13:31.802 --> 00:13:35.320
It's impossible for there to be
only Tonys walking around.

00:13:35.397 --> 00:13:37.505
In an office
with this many ponytails,

00:13:37.507 --> 00:13:40.584
- there's not one Furio?
- Who's Furio?

00:13:40.761 --> 00:13:42.410
He was the hit man from Italy

00:13:42.412 --> 00:13:44.187
who Carmela fell in love with
in season four.

00:13:44.264 --> 00:13:45.630
Oh, right, right, right.

00:13:45.758 --> 00:13:48.076
Now, now,
we've had a few hiccups,

00:13:48.101 --> 00:13:50.919
but I assure you,
the problem is solved.

00:13:50.921 --> 00:13:54.422
The machine is finally
giving accurate results.

00:13:54.424 --> 00:13:56.867
Oh, yeah? We'll see about that.

00:13:58.829 --> 00:14:00.771
"Meadow's boyfriend Finn"?

00:14:00.847 --> 00:14:02.588
But he's the worst one.

00:14:05.427 --> 00:14:09.762
Oh, my God, we've been faking
the results this whole time.

00:14:10.841 --> 00:14:13.099
-Tony?
-Oh, cool. I always thought
I was a Tony--

00:14:13.124 --> 00:14:16.066
No, no. This whole operation
has been a sham!

00:14:16.196 --> 00:14:17.621
And as for you...

00:14:17.698 --> 00:14:19.105
Why did you do it?

00:14:19.124 --> 00:14:22.200
Childhood abuse
or drug dependency,

00:14:22.277 --> 00:14:24.127
I'll-I'll see what my lawyer
tells me to say.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.136
Welcome back.
Our next show is The Bear

00:14:33.313 --> 00:14:35.313
because they wouldn't let us
do Pam &amp; Tommy

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:37.390
with Stewie as the penis.
Not a joke.

00:14:37.467 --> 00:14:40.652
Anyway, here's The Bear,
a show of which I am a huge fan.

00:14:40.728 --> 00:14:42.987
I'm the fan.
You've never even seen it.

00:14:43.064 --> 00:14:44.989
I know how to make a sandwich
and yell at people,

00:14:45.066 --> 00:14:46.899
it'll be fine.

00:14:52.332 --> 00:14:55.667
-Well, look who's back in town.
-That's right.

00:14:55.669 --> 00:14:58.169
Sure is weird being back
in the old family restaurant

00:14:58.246 --> 00:14:59.504
-here in Detroit--
-Chicago.

00:14:59.529 --> 00:15:01.287
Chicago, but I am super sad

00:15:01.341 --> 00:15:02.657
-about how my daughter--
-Brother.

00:15:02.659 --> 00:15:04.417
-Brother committed
grand larceny--
-Suicide.

00:15:04.494 --> 00:15:06.085
Yep. I-I only watched a trailer.

00:15:06.264 --> 00:15:09.497
Anyway, now that he's dead,
I can finally fulfill my dream

00:15:09.499 --> 00:15:11.499
of turning this place
into a Johnny Rockets

00:15:11.501 --> 00:15:13.777
and/or the fancy restaurant
from Ratatouille.

00:15:13.999 --> 00:15:16.354
I kind of feel like
you got to pick a direction.

00:15:16.431 --> 00:15:17.781
We're doing both.

00:15:17.858 --> 00:15:19.858
Great words won't cover
 ugly actions

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.911
Good frames won't save
 bad paintings

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:25.372
Whoo

00:15:27.367 --> 00:15:29.517
We lack the motion

00:15:29.519 --> 00:15:31.060
To move to the new.

00:15:31.085 --> 00:15:33.296
Yeah. You can tell
I'm good at cheffing

00:15:33.373 --> 00:15:35.357
because of all the quick edits
and loud music.

00:15:35.359 --> 00:15:37.375
Mm. The previous chef
only cooked

00:15:37.452 --> 00:15:39.302
with fewer edits
and quieter music,

00:15:39.379 --> 00:15:41.546
so, therefore,
I resent this change.

00:15:41.623 --> 00:15:44.457
Well, get on board 'cause
that's how we're doing it now.

00:15:44.534 --> 00:15:46.959
Bad news, Chef,
we're out of... I...

00:15:47.037 --> 00:15:48.703
I don't-- I want to say beef?

00:15:48.705 --> 00:15:50.147
Yes, beef.

00:15:50.223 --> 00:15:51.964
You people are unbelievable.

00:15:52.042 --> 00:15:53.391
Oh, boy, now what do I do?

00:15:53.468 --> 00:15:56.060
Well, if you've got
any oven jeans to sell,

00:15:56.137 --> 00:15:56.987
this is the time.

00:15:58.484 --> 00:16:02.467
You told me you had a supply
of vintage denim to sell.

00:16:02.469 --> 00:16:04.302
This is just a Winnie the Pooh
varsity jacket.

00:16:04.304 --> 00:16:07.011
You want it or not? I got a lot
of people who are interested.

00:16:07.069 --> 00:16:07.915
I...

00:16:09.202 --> 00:16:10.534
No. I don't want it.

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.252
What if I told you
there's condoms

00:16:12.328 --> 00:16:13.939
in every pocket of that bad boy?

00:16:14.382 --> 00:16:18.217
I'd say you just got yourself
40 pounds of trunk beef.

00:16:22.218 --> 00:16:24.960
Wow, Chef, you've really
turned this place upside down.

00:16:25.058 --> 00:16:28.242
Yes, even I have been
reluctantly won over.

00:16:28.319 --> 00:16:29.577
Well, it's just in time

00:16:29.654 --> 00:16:31.504
for the big Chicago food critic
who's coming.

00:16:31.581 --> 00:16:34.507
He's the biggest food critic
in all of Chicago.

00:16:34.636 --> 00:16:37.729
Oh, yeah, they call him
"Mr. Chicago Food Guy."

00:16:37.754 --> 00:16:40.847
Anyone who gets a bad review
from him is executed,

00:16:40.924 --> 00:16:42.665
Chicago-style.

00:16:42.742 --> 00:16:44.684
Guys, if you didn't want
to do The Bear,

00:16:44.761 --> 00:16:47.520
just say so,
but whatever this is...

00:16:47.597 --> 00:16:49.689
All I know is
this hectic kitchen of ours

00:16:49.808 --> 00:16:52.508
sure is different from the
fancy, hoity-toity restaurant

00:16:52.586 --> 00:16:53.893
in New York
where I used to work,

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.112
of which we will now see
a flashback.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.516
Is this food tiny enough
for rich New Yorkers, Chef?

00:17:00.594 --> 00:17:02.852
-Plate bigger,
food tinier, Chef.
-Yes, Chef.

00:17:02.929 --> 00:17:05.606
It should look like two grains
of rice served on a door.

00:17:05.631 --> 00:17:06.614
Yes, Chef.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:08.664
You call this tiny food?

00:17:08.689 --> 00:17:10.043
This food is enormous.

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:11.378
You're terrible at this, Chef.

00:17:11.454 --> 00:17:13.104
I'm sorry, Chef.
I'll do better, Chef.

00:17:13.106 --> 00:17:15.790
The woman at table 35 wants
a sausage for her buns.

00:17:15.994 --> 00:17:17.677
That was a sex joke,
say "giggity," Chef.

00:17:17.702 --> 00:17:19.293
Giggity, Chef.

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:21.412
You'll never make it in
the New York restaurant scene,

00:17:21.489 --> 00:17:23.506
which is understood
to be the fanciest even though

00:17:23.602 --> 00:17:24.860
when you say "New York,"
people immediately

00:17:24.918 --> 00:17:27.085
-think of street hot dogs.
-Yes, Chef.

00:17:27.161 --> 00:17:28.252
And where's your hair net?

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:30.162
Oh, I-I thought
it was sexy underwear.

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:31.756
-There's no leg holes.
-I know.

00:17:31.781 --> 00:17:34.257
I just bagged it
like grocery store onions.

00:17:35.262 --> 00:17:36.335
Juxtaposition.

00:17:36.629 --> 00:17:38.188
Anyway, I got us fresh sourdough

00:17:38.264 --> 00:17:39.856
for the big Chicago food critic.

00:17:39.933 --> 00:17:41.416
No, that was the old way.

00:17:41.418 --> 00:17:43.434
We're doing
just King's Hawaiian rolls.

00:17:43.459 --> 00:17:45.125
I think we should stick
with sourdough.

00:17:45.196 --> 00:17:46.378
No! Only cake bread!

00:17:46.572 --> 00:17:47.663
Wet beef on cake!

00:17:47.851 --> 00:17:49.084
That's my Chicago!

00:17:49.374 --> 00:17:51.092
Speaking of which, I think
it's time for several shots

00:17:51.094 --> 00:17:54.204
of an elevated train with a lot
of clickity-clack noises.

00:18:04.107 --> 00:18:05.548
Chicago!

00:18:08.511 --> 00:18:10.085
All right, big day today, Chefs.

00:18:10.269 --> 00:18:11.512
Since that critic is coming,

00:18:11.589 --> 00:18:13.731
everything needs
to go exactly right.

00:18:15.308 --> 00:18:16.017
Here he is!

00:18:16.193 --> 00:18:18.193
All right,
signed photo of Rick Moranis.

00:18:18.271 --> 00:18:19.546
Off to a good start.

00:18:19.622 --> 00:18:21.773
Welcome to the restaurant
from The Bear.

00:18:21.775 --> 00:18:23.457
We saved you our best table.

00:18:23.534 --> 00:18:25.276
Joe, what-what
are you doing here?

00:18:25.278 --> 00:18:27.295
Oh, I heard we were doing
a special episode,

00:18:27.371 --> 00:18:29.447
so I decided to drop in
as my favorite

00:18:29.449 --> 00:18:31.616
FX-next-day-on-Hulu character:

00:18:31.618 --> 00:18:35.887
Raylan Givens
from Justified: City Primeval.

00:18:35.912 --> 00:18:37.455
You can't just say
a bunch of words

00:18:37.457 --> 00:18:38.639
and think they make a sentence.

00:18:38.769 --> 00:18:40.989
Oh, you kidding me?
It's huge on Hulu.

00:18:41.014 --> 00:18:43.105
Top 100 for sure. Easy.

00:18:43.414 --> 00:18:44.562
At least top 200.

00:18:44.964 --> 00:18:46.239
-Easy.
-Joe, get out of here.

00:18:46.316 --> 00:18:48.800
I'm trying to get a baby critic
to like my wet beef.

00:18:48.802 --> 00:18:50.726
Oh, I'm so sorry,
then just say that.

00:18:50.931 --> 00:18:53.448
All right, good news, while
you two were messing around,

00:18:53.473 --> 00:18:54.806
I tried all the food.

00:18:54.808 --> 00:18:56.991
And I'm going to give
a great review to The Bear.

00:18:57.068 --> 00:18:59.401
Okay, time-out.
What is "The Bear"?

00:18:59.479 --> 00:19:00.812
Wha--?
Is this restaurant "The Bear"?

00:19:00.814 --> 00:19:03.314
-Am I "The Bear"?
-I-I thought the idea was

00:19:03.316 --> 00:19:06.075
restaurant ownership, you know,
more generally, is "a bear."

00:19:06.152 --> 00:19:08.502
Oh, okay, all right,
that makes sense.

00:19:08.504 --> 00:19:09.837
Chris, you've seen the show.

00:19:09.914 --> 00:19:11.672
Who or what is "The Bear"?

00:19:11.749 --> 00:19:14.267
Well, your last name
is "Berzatto,"

00:19:14.472 --> 00:19:16.398
so "bear."

00:19:16.496 --> 00:19:18.179
Okay, so it is me. I'm Bear.

00:19:18.255 --> 00:19:19.664
People call me "The Bear"?

00:19:19.666 --> 00:19:21.015
Yeah, they kind of don't though.

00:19:21.017 --> 00:19:23.334
But in season two,
there's a restaurant

00:19:23.336 --> 00:19:24.836
also called "The Bear."

00:19:24.838 --> 00:19:26.671
All right, so I'm feeling
like you have not answered

00:19:26.774 --> 00:19:28.457
my question at all,
but just so I'm clear:

00:19:28.482 --> 00:19:29.758
I'm the main guy, right?

00:19:29.783 --> 00:19:32.151
I-I'm the guy who sort of
looks like a hot Gene Wilder?

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.745
-Oh, does he?
-Yeah. Yeah, let's pull him up.

00:19:34.822 --> 00:19:35.838
See, look at that,
 can't you see,

00:19:35.915 --> 00:19:37.656
like, a kind of CrossFit
 Willy Wonka?

00:19:37.734 --> 00:19:39.342
No, no,
 we're not taking shots at hi.

00:19:39.419 --> 00:19:41.546
He's an FX-next-day-on-Hulu
 star.

00:19:41.769 --> 00:19:43.513
Let's bring up the MyPillow guy.

00:19:43.589 --> 00:19:46.999
He looks like a guy whose
 Discover card just bounced.

00:19:47.095 --> 00:19:49.279
Or a guy who takes
 solo trips to Thailand.

00:19:49.386 --> 00:19:52.296
That guy your mom dated
 too soon after the divorce.

00:19:52.298 --> 00:19:55.149
The driver's ed instructor
 you've heard whispers about.

00:19:55.226 --> 00:19:58.302
He's definitely burned
 his hand on a Benihana grill.

00:19:58.304 --> 00:20:00.913
The reason nine women
 have just broken their silenc.

00:20:01.273 --> 00:20:02.974
He looks
 like he belongs in the front

00:20:02.976 --> 00:20:04.475
and back seat of a police car.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:06.243
He looks like a guy
 who boards in Group 3

00:20:06.268 --> 00:20:07.920
even though he's in Group 8.

00:20:07.997 --> 00:20:09.756
He looks like a guy
 who's not allowed to talk

00:20:09.832 --> 00:20:11.090
to his wife's sister.

00:20:11.167 --> 00:20:12.592
Or a male nurse that interrupts

00:20:12.668 --> 00:20:14.502
a family's final moments.

00:20:14.776 --> 00:20:16.689
Maybe this,
maybe this is "The Bear."

00:20:16.847 --> 00:20:19.681
I don't get why you're dead-set
against doing this show.

00:20:19.759 --> 00:20:21.033
What are you afraid of?

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:23.352
I don't know. I'm-I'm just
confused by all of it.

00:20:23.429 --> 00:20:25.687
When I was a kid,
it was just ladies that cooked.

00:20:25.765 --> 00:20:27.932
And guys with tattoos
lived on ships.

00:20:27.934 --> 00:20:29.493
Okay?
That's what makes sense to me.

00:20:29.808 --> 00:20:31.082
This has been Da Bear.

00:20:37.539 --> 00:20:38.892
Well, we hope
you had fun tonight

00:20:38.969 --> 00:20:40.711
with some of Hulu's
best offerings.

00:20:40.963 --> 00:20:42.396
I know we sure did.

00:20:42.525 --> 00:20:44.709
But before we go,
let's take a moment to remember

00:20:44.734 --> 00:20:47.568
some of the streaming services
that are no longer with us.

00:20:47.898 --> 00:20:52.585
I will remember you

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:59.331
Will you remember me?